{
  "id": "course_32_la_guerre_du_vietnam_1955_1975",
  "title": "Why didn't the United States win in Vietnam?",
  "subtitle": "1955-1975",
  "subject": "histoire",
  "subcategory": "The Cold War & today's world",
  "description": "For twenty years the United States poured enormous force into Vietnam to hold back communism. It got bogged down, tore itself apart, and left without winning.",
  "hero": {
    "image": "la_guerre_du_vietnam",
    "ratio": "16:9",
    "hook": "The world's leading power, stuck in a war it could not win."
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "course_32_la_guerre_du_vietnam_1955_1975_intro",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "free": true,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "At the height of the war, **543,000** American soldiers are in Vietnam. And yet the world's leading power does not win. Twenty years of war, millions of dead, and in the end its opponent comes out on top. How is that possible?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "vietnam_huey_helicopters_1966",
          "ratio": "1280:827",
          "caption": "Look at this photo from 1966. It shows how America fights.",
          "stops": [
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 1,
              "text": "Look at this photo from 1966. It shows how America fights."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.78,
              "y": 0.52,
              "zoom": 1.7,
              "text": "**The helicopter.** Soldiers call it the “Huey.” The United States sends nearly 12,000 helicopters to Vietnam."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.36,
              "y": 0.63,
              "zoom": 2.2,
              "text": "**The infantry.** Packs on their backs, they run to board. Within minutes they will be dropped deep in the jungle."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.27,
              "y": 0.49,
              "zoom": 2.4,
              "text": "**Behind them, more aircraft are waiting.** Troops arrive in waves, anywhere, at any time.",
              "outline": [
                {
                  "ellipse": [
                    0.385,
                    0.455,
                    0.075,
                    0.075
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "ellipse": [
                    0.145,
                    0.525,
                    0.03,
                    0.03
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 1,
              "text": "Speed, power, technology. On paper, nothing can stand up to this."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "course_32_la_guerre_du_vietnam_1955_1975_p1",
      "title": "A country cut in two",
      "free": false,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Since the 19th century, Vietnam has been a French colony. In **1954**, after eight years of fighting, France loses the [[First Indochina War]] and leaves."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "ho_chi_minh_portrait_1946",
          "ratio": "800:801",
          "caption": "Ho Chi Minh, around 1946. A communist and a nationalist, he led the fight against France. Now he wants a united, independent Vietnam."
        },
        {
          "type": "map",
          "map": "map_vietnam",
          "ratio": "5:6",
          "text": "Geneva, 1954. The great powers meet to decide the country's fate. They cut it in two at the 17th parallel. The split is meant to be temporary: elections are planned for 1956 to reunite the country. In the North, Ho Chi Minh leads a communist government, backed by the USSR and China. Its capital: Hanoi. In the South, an authoritarian, anti-communist government, backed by the United States. Its capital: Saigon. Two Vietnams, two sides of the Cold War. Each wants to reunite the country on its own terms.",
