        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The elections never happen: the South and Washington know Ho Chi Minh would win them. In the late 1950s, communist fighters take up arms in the South. Their enemies call them the [[Viet Cong]]."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Washington is worried. In 1954, President Eisenhower sums up his fear with a simple picture: the [[domino theory]]. If Vietnam falls, its neighbors will fall one after another."
        },
        {
          "type": "question",
          "kind": "choice",
          "prompt": "So **why** does the United States get involved in Vietnam?",
          "options": [
            "To take over the former French colony for itself.",
            "Out of fear that communism will spread, one by one, to neighboring countries.",
            "To get its hands on the region's oil."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "That is the domino theory. Washington does not want a colony: it wants an allied South Vietnam, so the rest of Asia does not tip over."
        }
      ]
