    {
      "id": "course_32_la_guerre_du_vietnam_1955_1975_p2",
      "title": "Sliding into war",
      "free": false,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "At first, America only sends military advisers: about **16,000** under President Kennedy. Then, on **August 2, 1964**, an American ship, the *Maddox*, is sailing off the northern coast, in the Gulf of Tonkin."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "gulf_of_tonkin_torpedo_boats_1964",
          "ratio": "2400:1436",
          "caption": "This photo is taken that day from the deck of the Maddox. At first glance, an empty sea.",
          "stops": [
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 1,
              "text": "This photo is taken that day from the deck of the *Maddox*. At first glance, an empty sea."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.73,
              "y": 0.37,
              "zoom": 3,
              "text": "**On the horizon, two dark dots.** They are North Vietnamese torpedo boats, racing toward the ship.",
              "outline": [
                {
                  "ellipse": [
                    0.632,
                    0.365,
                    0.028,
                    0.04
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "ellipse": [
                    0.822,
                    0.36,
                    0.028,
                    0.04
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "x": 0.09,
              "y": 0.38,
              "zoom": 3,
              "text": "**A third one, further left.** They fire their torpedoes, miss, and pull back under American fire.",
              "outline": [
                {
                  "ellipse": [
                    0.048,
                    0.372,
                    0.028,
                    0.04
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 1,
              "text": "That day, the attack is real. Two days later, things get murky."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "On **August 4**, President Johnson is told of a second attack. It is night, the sea is rough, and the ships' messages contradict each other. Johnson does not wait: he orders air strikes on the North."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Three days later, Congress gives him, almost unanimously, the right to use force. We now know the second attack **never happened**."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "johnson_signs_tonkin_resolution_1964",
          "ratio": "1000:783",
          "caption": "August 10, 1964. Johnson signs the text passed by Congress. He can now wage war without ever declaring it."
        },
        {
          "type": "funFact",
          "text": "Johnson himself had doubts. A few days later he reportedly said in private: “For all I know, our Navy was shooting at whales out there.”"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "In **March 1965**, everything speeds up. American planes bomb the North almost without pause: this is Operation [[Rolling Thunder]]. The first combat troops land on the beach at Da Nang."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "marine_da_nang_beach_1965",
          "ratio": "900:949",
          "caption": "Da Nang, August 1965. A young Marine waits on the beach during a landing."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "mcnamara_vietnam_map_press_conference_1965",
          "ratio": "2000:1328",
          "caption": "Washington, April 1965. A press conference about the war.",
          "stops": [
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 1,
              "text": "Washington, April 1965. A press conference about the war."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.17,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 2.1,
              "text": "**Robert McNamara**, the U.S. Secretary of Defense. A former head of Ford, he believes in numbers, maps and charts."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.33,
              "y": 0.56,
              "zoom": 2,
              "text": "**On the board**, a title: “North Vietnam bridge interdiction.” Each circle is an aerial photo of a targeted bridge."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.78,
              "y": 0.48,
              "zoom": 1.9,
              "text": "**On the map**, his pointer follows Vietnam. His idea: cut from the air the roads that supply the South, until the North gives in."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "question",
          "kind": "truefalse",
          "prompt": "The bombing of the North was planned for **eight weeks**. It lasted more than **three years**.",
          "options": [
            "True",
            "False"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "From March 1965 to November 1968. The North repairs its bridges, scatters its depots, and does not give in."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "course_32_la_guerre_du_vietnam_1955_1975_p3",
      "title": "Why force is not enough",
      "free": false,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "In three years, America goes from **23,000** to more than **500,000** soldiers on the ground. Its method has a name: “search and destroy.” Helicopters drop the troops, they hunt the enemy, then they leave."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "On the other side, General **Giap**, head of the northern army, refuses the big battle. His men strike, then vanish into the jungle, the villages or tunnels. Ground won in the morning is lost again by night."
        },
        {
          "type": "map",
          "map": "map_vietnam",
          "ratio": "5:6",
          "text": "To hold on, fighters in the South need weapons and men from the North. But the border at the 17th parallel is watched. So the North goes around it. Thousands of kilometers of tracks cross Laos and Cambodia, two neighboring countries: this is the Ho Chi Minh Trail. American planes bomb it relentlessly. Every night it is repaired. The supplies never stop.",
