    {
      "id": "course_32_la_guerre_du_vietnam_1955_1975_p5",
      "title": "The end, and what it leaves behind",
      "free": false,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Elected in late 1968, President Nixon changes the plan: bring American soldiers home step by step and let the southern army fight alone. This is [[Vietnamization]]. The war still lasts four more years, and spills into Cambodia and Laos."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "paris_peace_accords_signing_1973",
          "ratio": "1100:744",
          "caption": "Paris, January 27, 1973. After more than four years of talks, the United States signs its way out of Vietnam."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Without its protector, the South holds on for two years. In the spring of **1975**, the northern army launches its final assault. On **April 30**, its tanks roll into Saigon."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "saigon_evacuation_refugees_1975",
          "ratio": "696:564",
          "caption": "April 29, 1975. Saigon is about to fall. In less than two days, the Americans evacuate more than 7,000 people by helicopter.",
          "stops": [
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 1,
              "text": "April 29, 1975. Saigon is about to fall. In less than two days, the Americans evacuate more than 7,000 people by helicopter."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.45,
              "y": 0.12,
              "zoom": 1.5,
              "text": "**Behind them, the helicopter** that has just dropped them on an American ship, out at sea."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.38,
              "y": 0.8,
              "zoom": 1.9,
              "text": "**One bag in hand.** It is all they could take. In the years that follow, hundreds of thousands more will flee the country by sea."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Vietnam is reunited under a communist government, and Saigon becomes Ho Chi Minh City. The toll is terrible: between one and three million Vietnamese killed, civilians and soldiers."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "vietnam_veterans_memorial_wall",
          "ratio": "1280:853",
          "caption": "Washington, today. This black granite wall is the memorial to the American soldiers of Vietnam.",
          "stops": [
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 1,
              "text": "Washington, today. This black granite wall is the memorial to the American soldiers of Vietnam."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.08,
              "y": 0.1,
              "zoom": 2.7,
              "text": "**Names carved in the stone.** More than 58,000: every American killed or missing in the war."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.53,
              "y": 0.44,
              "zoom": 2.3,
              "text": "**At the end of the path, the Washington Monument.** The wall points toward it: the defeat is written into the heart of the capital.",
              "outline": [
                {
                  "ellipse": [
                    0.535,
                    0.455,
                    0.022,
                    0.085
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "In the United States, people will long speak of the “Vietnam syndrome”: the fear of getting bogged down again in a distant war that force alone cannot win."
        },
